.TH CJSON_TYPE_STR 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cjson_type_str \- name a DOM node's type as a printable string
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cjson.h>

static inline const char *cjson_type_str(cjson node);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the spelling of the type tag of node as a string. It is for
diagnostics, such as a message that tells the reader that a value
did not have the type which a caller expected. Use cjson_type(3) to
read the tag itself. This function only makes the text for it.

The returned text is the enumerator's own name, one of:

    CJSON_NULL         JSON null literal
    CJSON_BOOL         JSON true or false
    CJSON_INTEGER      JSON number with no decimal point or exponent
    CJSON_FLOAT        JSON number with a decimal point or an exponent
    CJSON_STRING       JSON string
    CJSON_LIST         JSON array
    CJSON_DICTIONARY   JSON object

A NULL node gives "CJSON_NULL", because cjson_type(3) reports
CJSON_NULL for it. A tag that is not in cjson_node_type_t gives
"CJSON_UNKNOWN". A valid node never carries such a tag.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
node  Node to describe. May be NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A pointer to a string literal naming the node's type. Never NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The result has static storage duration and lives for the whole
program. The caller can therefore store it and print it later. The
caller must not change it and must not free it.

This result alone does not tell a NULL node apart from a true JSON
null node. Compare the handle against NULL first when the caller
needs that difference.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cjson doc = cjson_parse("{\e"k\e":1}", NULL);
cjson k = cjson_get(doc, "k");

if (cjson_type(k) != CJSON_STRING) {
    fprintf(stderr, "k is %s, expected CJSON_STRING\en",
            cjson_type_str(k));
}

cjson_destroy(doc);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_type (3),
.BR cjson_bool_val (3),
.BR cjson_int_val (3),
.BR cjson_double_val (3),
.BR cjson_str_val (3)
